  "content": {
    "id": "p29-q17-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "17",
    "prompt": "A cohort of $n$ students are to be given $k$ prefect roles, which includes a school captain position.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p29-q17-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "By considering the number of ways to fill the roles, show that\n$$k\\binom{n}{k}=n\\binom{n-1}{k-1}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$k\\binom{n}{k}=n\\binom{n-1}{k-1}$.\n\nChoose the prefects then their captain, or choose the captain then the remaining prefects.",
          "worked": "Choose the $k$ prefects in $\\binom{n}{k}$ ways, then their captain in $k$ ways. This gives $k\\binom{n}{k}$ outcomes.\n\nAlternatively, choose the captain in $n$ ways, then choose the other $k-1$ prefects from the remaining $n-1$ students in $\\binom{n-1}{k-1}$ ways.\n\nBoth methods count the same prefect group with its captain, so\n$$k\\binom{n}{k}=n\\binom{n-1}{k-1}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q17-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, use the identity to prove that\n$$1\\binom{n}{1}+2\\binom{n}{2}+3\\binom{n}{3}+\\cdots+n\\binom{n}{n}=n2^{n-1}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1\\binom{n}{1}+2\\binom{n}{2}+\\cdots+n\\binom{n}{n}=n2^{n-1}$.\n\nApply part (a) to each term, factor out $n$, and use the binomial sum $2^{n-1}$.",
          "worked": "Apply part (a) to each term and factor out $n$:\n$$\\begin{align*}1\\binom{n}{1}+2\\binom{n}{2}+\\cdots+n\\binom{n}{n}&=n\\binom{n-1}{0}+n\\binom{n-1}{1}+\\cdots+n\\binom{n-1}{n-1}\\\\1\\binom{n}{1}+2\\binom{n}{2}+\\cdots+n\\binom{n}{n}&=n\\left[\\binom{n-1}{0}+\\binom{n-1}{1}+\\cdots+\\binom{n-1}{n-1}\\right]\\\\1\\binom{n}{1}+2\\binom{n}{2}+\\cdots+n\\binom{n}{n}&=n2^{n-1}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe bracketed sum is $2^{n-1}$ by the taught light-switch identity with $n-1$ switches.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      }
    ]
  },
